no longer reproduces on `main`, stop and write that to `FINDINGS.md` — a stale phase must never be "fixed" by inventing a change.  ## Goal  Add a logging facility with levels, a settable threshold, a settable destination, and a timestamp on each record, so a library can log without speaking to stdout. Make the default level quiet enough that adding a log call cannot change a program's observable output, since a library that changes its caller's output is a defect. Decide whether timestamps make records non-deterministic, and if so keep them out of anything a test would compare - the corpus compares output literally, so a timestamp in the default path would break it.  ## Method  1. Read `AGENTS.md` in full. Obey every hard rule; they are not negotiable. 2. Reproduce the finding. Record the exact command and the exact wrong output. 3. Write the failing test FIRST. Watch it fail. Do not touch production code    until you have a red test that fails for the right reason. 4. Make the smallest change that turns it green. 5. Run the four gates yourself before claiming anything:     ```bash    cargo fmt --all -- --check    cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings    cargo test --all-targets    ./rbops/verify.sh phase-103    ```  6. Write `REPORT.md` in the exact shape required by `AGENTS.md` section 4.  ## Definition of done  - [ ] a record at or above the threshold is emitted and one below it is not, asserted by capturing the destination, for every adjacent pair of levels - [ ] the default level emits nothing, so adding a log call to a library cannot change a program's observable output - asserted by a corpus-style golden comparison - [ ] the destination is settable to a file and a record written there is readable back with the level and message intact - [ ] a timestamp is present on each record and a test proves a deterministic mode exists that omits it, so corpus goldens stay stable - [ ] logging to a closed or unwritable destination raises a catchable error rather than 